/*!

\page cartesiangridplugin_algorithm_page How the algorithm works

This page explains what happens inside the <b>Cartesian Grid 3D</b> algorithm. You don't need it to use the plug-in,
but it helps to understand the messages, the timings and the limits.

The algorithm handles all the solids of the main shape together, in one pass. It does not need a surface mesh first
(unlike algorithms that mesh the faces before the volume), and it does not support sub-meshes. In these respects it
behaves like the Body Fitting algorithm of SMESH.

<h2>The steps of a compute</h2>

-# <b>Clean up.</b> If an earlier compute left cells attached to the main shape (cells outside the solids kept with the
   \b Keep option), they are removed first. This is done even if the new compute fails later.
-# <b>Check the inputs.</b> The algorithm must be assigned to every solid of the main shape. The \b Keep option needs a
   main shape that is a compound. Every solid must be closed. The solids are then sorted from the smallest volume to the
   largest, for the "smallest solid wins" rule.
-# <b>Build the grid.</b> The bounding box is computed from the solids only, so construction points and faces do not
   make the grid bigger. The hypothesis then gives the positions of the grid lines along each axis. If the grid is too
   large to be stored in memory, it is refused at this point, before anything is allocated. If grid lines given as
   coordinates cut a solid, a warning says so.
-# <b>Find the owner of each cell.</b> For every cell, the algorithm finds the smallest solid containing its center, or
   notes that the cell is outside all solids. This is the most delicate step; it is explained in
   \ref cartesiangridplugin_scanline "The scanline classifier" below.
-# <b>Create the mesh.</b> The cells are visited one layer at a time (k), one row at a time (j), one cell at a time (i).
   The cells that have an owner solid are created first, then the kept outside cells. Neighbouring hexahedra share
   their corner nodes. Each hexahedron is attached to its owner solid, or to the main shape for cells outside all
   solids.
-# <b>Finish.</b> A solid that got no cell produces a warning (not an error). Other warnings report the number of kept
   outside cells, the cell centers that the geometry kernel could not locate, and the solids cut by coordinate grid
   lines.

Three related commands:
- <b>Evaluate</b> runs the same checks and visits the cells in the same order, but creates nothing. This gives the exact
  number of nodes and hexahedra for each solid, before you compute.
- <b>Cancel</b>: the algorithm checks for a cancel request after each row of cells, while finding the owners and while
  creating the mesh. A canceled compute removes everything it had created, so no half-built mesh is left behind.
- <b>Progress</b>: finding the owners counts for 20 % of the progress bar, and creating the nodes and hexahedra for
  80 %, because that is roughly how the time is shared.

\anchor cartesiangridplugin_scanline
<h2>The scanline classifier</h2>

"Classifying" a cell means finding which solid contains its center. This section explains how the plug-in does it fast.

<h3>The slow way: one test per cell</h3>

The geometry kernel of SALOME (OpenCASCADE) can tell whether a point is inside a solid: this is the class
<code>BRepClass3d_SolidClassifier</code>. To answer, it sends a ray from the point and computes where the ray crosses
the faces of the solid, using the exact surfaces (planes, spheres, B-splines...). This is an expensive calculation.

The simple method tests every cell center against the solids, from the smallest to the largest, until one solid
contains the point. A grid of n &times; n &times; n cells then needs n<sup>3</sup> such tests. On a sphere, with 12
threads, each test costs about 4 microseconds, so 10<sup>6</sup> cells take about 4 seconds, and the time grows very
quickly with n.

<h3>The idea: test whole rows of cells</h3>

"Scanline" is a term from computer graphics: instead of handling a picture one point at a time, you handle it one line
at a time. To fill a polygon on a screen, for example, you take one row of pixels, compute where this row crosses the
edges of the polygon, and fill everything between the first and second crossing, between the third and fourth, and so
on. No pixel needs its own inside/outside test.

The plug-in does the same in 3D. A row of cells along X (fixed j and k) plays the role of the row of pixels, and the
faces of the solid play the role of the polygon edges.

Along a row, a cell center can only change from inside to outside (or the reverse) where the row's line crosses the
surface of the solid. Between two crossings, all the points of the line are on the same side. So, for each row and each
solid, the plug-in:
- computes once where the line crosses the surface of the solid, which gives a short sorted list of crossing positions;
- tests one point per <b>stretch</b> of line between two crossings, at the middle of the stretch, and only if a cell
  needs it;
- tests separately, one by one, the cells whose center is closer than one cell width to a crossing. Near a crossing,
  the shortcut above is less reliable (the line may touch the surface, cross it on an edge, or pass very close to a
  cell center), so these cells get the exact test.

Every other cell simply takes the answer of its stretch. Finding the stretch of a cell is a quick search in the short
list of crossings: no geometry is involved.

\image html cartesiangrid_scanline.png "A horizontal cut through a sphere. On the highlighted row, the line along X crosses the surface twice. The cells whose center is less than one cell width from a crossing get the exact test (orange). The cells in between take the answer of a single test at the middle of their stretch (green dot). Cells outside the box around the solid are skipped without any test." width=640px

Here is an example: a row through a sphere of radius 10, centered at the origin. The line starts a little before the
box around the solid and ends a little after it. It crosses the surface of the sphere at x = -9.9 and x = +9.9, which
cuts the line into three stretches:

\code
start ------- -9.9 ======================= +9.9 ------- end
 stretch 0           stretch 1                stretch 2
 middle ~ -10.4      middle = 0               middle ~ +10.4
\endcode

The line cannot go from inside to outside without crossing the surface, and every crossing is the end of a stretch. So
all the points of a stretch are on the same side, and one test, at any point of the stretch, gives the answer for all
the cells whose center is in it. The middle is chosen because it is the point farthest from both crossings: there, the
test is least likely to fall right on the surface, where the answer is uncertain. In this example, 100 cells inside the
sphere cost one test instead of 100.

<h3>Why this is so much faster</h3>

With n cells along each axis, the grid has n<sup>3</sup> cells but only n<sup>2</sup> rows. The expensive operations of
the scanline method (one line crossing per row, a few tests per row, and the tests near the surface) grow like the
number of rows, n<sup>2</sup>, instead of the number of cells, n<sup>3</sup>. When n doubles, the simple method becomes
8 times slower, and the scanline method only 4 times slower.

<table>
<tr><th>Work, for n cells per axis and S solids</th><th>One test per cell</th><th>Scanline</th></tr>
<tr><td>Line crossings</td><td>none</td><td>at most n<sup>2</sup> &times; S (one per row and per solid)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tests near the surface</td><td>-</td><td>about as many as cells cut by the surface, which grows like
n<sup>2</sup></td></tr>
<tr><td>Tests at the middle of stretches</td><td>-</td><td>a few per row and per solid: grows like n<sup>2</sup>
&times; S</td></tr>
<tr><td>Total number of point tests</td><td>up to n<sup>3</sup> &times; S</td><td>grows like n<sup>2</sup> &times;
S</td></tr>
<tr><td>Simple bookkeeping per cell</td><td>n<sup>3</sup></td><td>n<sup>3</sup> quick searches, a few nanoseconds
each</td></tr>
</table>

Only the simple bookkeeping still grows like n<sup>3</sup>, and it involves no geometry, so it remains negligible in the
measurements below, up to 8 million cells.

<h3>The result is exactly the same as with one test per cell</h3>

The shortcut is only used where it is safe. Everything else falls back to the exact test:
- The cells close to a crossing (less than one cell width away) always get the exact test.
- If the test at the middle of a stretch lands on the surface or gives no clear answer, all the cells of that stretch
  get the exact test.
- If the line crossing computation fails for a row, the whole row gets one exact test per cell.
- A line passing through an edge between two faces finds the same crossing twice, once per face. Crossings closer than
  a thousandth of the smallest cell size are merged into one, so that a stretch is not wrongly split in two.
- The "smallest solid wins" rule is kept: the solids are handled from the smallest to the largest, and a cell that
  already has an owner is skipped. A row stops early once all its cells have an owner, which saves time with nested
  volumes.
- Rows that do not cross the box around a solid skip that solid completely. Within a row, only the cells inside the box
  are visited.
- When a test cannot decide, it is tried again at a point moved very slightly inside the same cell. If it still cannot
  decide, the cell is considered outside that solid, and a warning gives the number of such cells.

The plug-in's tests compare the two methods on 8 shapes and 3 grids, and require exactly the same owner for every
cell. The grids include ones with different numbers of cells per axis (17 &times; 19 &times; 23, 31 &times; 8 &times;
13). The shapes are a sphere, a torus, a tilted cylinder, a cone (with its tip), a box with a hole, a rotated box, a
compound of nested and overlapping solids, and two boxes touching on a face whose faces lie exactly on cell centers.

<h3>Using all the processor cores</h3>

The grid is split into layers along Z, and the layers are handed out to one worker thread per processor core: when a
thread finishes a layer, it takes the next free one. The OpenCASCADE objects that do the tests cannot be shared between
threads, so each thread creates its own, once per solid, the first time it needs them. If a thread hits an error, the
others stop, and the compute reports that error.

<h2>Measurements</h2>

These timings cover only the step that finds the owner of each cell, with 12 threads, on a grid of n &times; n &times; n
cells covering the bounding box, with an optimized (Release) build. Two test shapes: a sphere of radius 10, and a box
containing a sphere and a torus (three nested and overlapping solids).

\image html cartesiangrid_bench.png "Time to find the owner of every cell, on logarithmic scales. On such a plot, the slope of a line is the power of n: one test per cell (EXACT) has slope 3 (time grows like n cubed), the scanline method slope 2 (like n squared). The simple method was not run for n = 200; it would take about 30 s." width=640px

<table>
<tr><th>Shape</th><th>n</th><th>Cells</th><th>One test per cell (s)</th><th>Scanline (s)</th><th>Times faster</th></tr>
<tr><td>sphere</td><td>25</td><td>15 625</td><td>0.069</td><td>0.016</td><td>4.4</td></tr>
<tr><td>sphere</td><td>50</td><td>125 000</td><td>0.508</td><td>0.060</td><td>8.5</td></tr>
<tr><td>sphere</td><td>100</td><td>1 000 000</td><td>3.99</td><td>0.240</td><td>16.6</td></tr>
<tr><td>sphere</td><td>150</td><td>3 375 000</td><td>13.55</td><td>0.547</td><td>24.8</td></tr>
<tr><td>sphere</td><td>200</td><td>8 000 000</td><td>not run</td><td>0.975</td><td>-</td></tr>
<tr><td>box + sphere + torus</td><td>25</td><td>15 625</td><td>0.083</td><td>0.019</td><td>4.4</td></tr>
<tr><td>box + sphere + torus</td><td>50</td><td>125 000</td><td>0.630</td><td>0.063</td><td>10.0</td></tr>
<tr><td>box + sphere + torus</td><td>100</td><td>1 000 000</td><td>5.10</td><td>0.260</td><td>19.6</td></tr>
<tr><td>box + sphere + torus</td><td>150</td><td>3 375 000</td><td>16.87</td><td>0.573</td><td>29.4</td></tr>
<tr><td>box + sphere + torus</td><td>200</td><td>8 000 000</td><td>not run</td><td>1.046</td><td>-</td></tr>
</table>

The gain grows with the size of the grid: it roughly doubles each time n doubles, as expected from n<sup>3</sup>
divided by n<sup>2</sup>.

For a full compute with <code>mesh.Compute()</code> (12 threads, creation of the nodes and hexahedra included):

<table>
<tr><th>Case</th><th>One test per cell</th><th>Scanline</th></tr>
<tr><td>box, 10<sup>6</sup> cells</td><td>6.5 s</td><td>1.56 s</td></tr>
<tr><td>sphere, 150 &times; 150 &times; 150 cells</td><td>13.8 s</td><td>2.95 s</td></tr>
</table>

Finding the owners now takes only about 20 % of a compute. The remaining 80 % is spent creating the SMESH nodes and
hexahedra: one element per cell, on a single thread, because the SMESH data structure cannot be filled by several
threads at once. This is also why the \ref cartesiangridplugin_export_page "Cartesian MED export", which creates no
SMESH element, is much faster than a compute on large grids.

*/
